# mysqldump — real-world examples

## Dump a database to a file

```sh
# Password on the command line appears in shell history — prefer the prompt
mysqldump -uusername -ppassword the-database > db.sql

# Prompt for the password instead
mysqldump -uusername -p the-database > db.sql
```

## Dump a database compressed

```sh
mysqldump -uusername -p the-database | gzip -9 > db.sql.gz
```

## Dump all databases

```sh
mysqldump -uusername -p --all-databases > all-databases.sql
```

## Structure only / data only

```sh
# Schema only, no rows
mysqldump --no-data -uusername -p the-database > schema.sql

# Rows only, no CREATE statements
mysqldump --no-create-info -uusername -p the-database > data.sql
```

## Homelab: nightly dated backup of everything

```sh
mysqldump -u root -p --all-databases --single-transaction \
  | gzip > /srv/backups/daily/mariadb-$(date +%F).sql.gz
```

## Homelab: dump one table, restore a database

```sh
mysqldump -u david -p homelab_inventory hosts > hosts.sql
mysql -u david -p homelab_inventory < hosts.sql
```

| Flag                 | Meaning                                     |
| -------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| --all-databases      | dump every database                         |
| --no-data            | schema only                                 |
| --no-create-info     | data only                                   |
| --single-transaction | consistent InnoDB dump without locking      |
| --routines           | include stored procedures and functions     |
